WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.580
What does Finpoint do?

00:00:02.180 --> 00:00:03.111
The problem

00:00:03.411 --> 00:00:07.042
Your bank shows that money moved, but not what it was really for.

00:00:07.222 --> 00:00:10.698
One supermarket payment might be groceries and a gift.

00:00:10.878 --> 00:00:13.795
One dinner payment might include your friends’ shares.

00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:15.588
The idea

00:00:15.888 --> 00:00:23.078
Finpoint links each bank amount to your own explanations, then turns those explanations into spending and plans.

00:00:23.258 --> 00:00:25.454
You only need one purchase to start.

00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:27.266
Step 1

00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:29.356
Try the sample lunch lesson.

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:31.186
Step 2

00:00:31.486 --> 00:00:34.519
Import a bank CSV, or restore a backup.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.372
Step 3

00:00:36.672 --> 00:00:38.240
Explain one Statement.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:40.079
Step 4

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:41.668
Download a backup.

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:43.697
Check your result

00:00:43.997 --> 00:00:47.293
You can say where one bank amount went, and find it again.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:49.125
Watch out

00:00:49.425 --> 00:00:51.850
An empty Dashboard is normal at first.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:55.447
Imported Statements don’t count until you explain them.

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.160
Example

00:00:57.460 --> 00:00:59.609
Alex paid $12 for lunch.

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:01.948
The bank shows −$12.

00:01:02.128 --> 00:01:08.608
Alex adds a Lunch Record for −$12, and an Allocation of −$12 connects them.

00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:12.548
Statements, Records and Allocations

00:01:13.148 --> 00:01:14.079
The problem

00:01:14.379 --> 00:01:18.674
Statements and real-life purchases don’t always match one for one.

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:20.455
The idea

00:01:20.755 --> 00:01:23.493
A Statement is one entry of bank activity.

00:01:23.673 --> 00:01:26.401
A Record is your explanation of what happened.

00:01:26.581 --> 00:01:30.173
An Allocation links an amount from a Statement to a Record.

00:01:30.353 --> 00:01:33.102
An Account is where money sits, not your login.

00:01:33.282 --> 00:01:36.821
Money paid is negative, and money received is positive.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.633
Step 1

00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:41.310
Read the Statement’s amount and description.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:43.120
Step 2

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.150
Decide what it really was.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:46.972
Step 3

00:01:47.272 --> 00:01:49.273
Create a Record that explains it.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:51.112
Step 4

00:01:51.412 --> 00:01:52.685
Allocate the amount.

00:01:52.865 --> 00:01:58.081
One Statement can feed several Records, and one Record can combine several Statements.

00:01:58.933 --> 00:02:00.097
Check your result

00:02:00.397 --> 00:02:07.982
A Record is complete when it has an Allocation, its Allocation total equals its amount, and none of its Statements are Pending.

00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:09.792
Watch out

00:02:10.092 --> 00:02:12.810
Two Statements don’t always mean two Records.

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:16.789
A payment and its repayment can explain one shared purchase.

00:02:17.667 --> 00:02:18.526
Example

00:02:18.826 --> 00:02:25.324
Statement −$12, Allocation −$12, Lunch Record −$12.

00:02:25.504 --> 00:02:29.560
Your Account balance and your Dashboard spending answer different questions.

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.967
Starting fresh, or restoring

00:02:33.567 --> 00:02:34.498
The problem

00:02:34.798 --> 00:02:40.240
A new workspace comes with starter Categories and spending buckets, but no bank activity yet.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:42.022
The idea

00:02:42.322 --> 00:02:45.386
Everything is stored in this browser, on this device.

00:02:45.566 --> 00:02:47.907
The starter defaults are yours to edit.

00:02:48.087 --> 00:02:52.628
You can learn with practice lessons first, or restore a backup you already have.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.433
Step 1

00:02:54.733 --> 00:02:55.574
New here?

00:02:55.754 --> 00:02:57.765
Open Importer and choose your bank.

00:02:58.633 --> 00:02:59.586
Step 2

00:02:59.886 --> 00:03:00.947
Have a backup?

00:03:01.127 --> 00:03:02.850
Open Sync and restore it.

00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:04.705
Step 3

00:03:05.005 --> 00:03:08.117
Export your current data first if you want to keep it.

00:03:08.297 --> 00:03:09.896
Restoring replaces it;

00:03:10.076 --> 00:03:11.184
it doesn’t merge.

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:13.045
Step 4

00:03:13.345 --> 00:03:16.989
Use Help in the top bar any time, without leaving a form.

00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:19.031
Check your result

00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:23.352
Your Accounts, Statements or restored Records show up in their lists.

00:03:24.233 --> 00:03:25.192
Watch out

00:03:25.492 --> 00:03:28.004
Load demo data is not a tutorial.

00:03:28.184 --> 00:03:29.691
It replaces your data.

00:03:29.871 --> 00:03:31.899
Use the practice lessons instead.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:33.626
Example

00:03:33.926 --> 00:03:41.469
In practice, you can explain a −$12 lunch without uploading anything or changing your real Account balance.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.720
Importing bank activity

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:46.251
The problem

00:03:46.551 --> 00:03:50.439
Finpoint can only read a bank file in a format it supports.

00:03:50.619 --> 00:03:52.919
Each row becomes one of your Statements.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:54.722
The idea

00:03:55.022 --> 00:04:01.402
Importer supports CSV exports from DBS, OCBC, UOB and Revolut.

00:04:01.582 --> 00:04:04.168
It creates Statements, not finished Records.

00:04:04.348 --> 00:04:06.869
Missing Accounts are created as you go.

00:04:07.049 --> 00:04:11.500
Revolut takes one file at a time, and asks which Account it belongs to.

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:13.299
Step 1

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:15.572
Export a CSV from your bank.

00:04:15.752 --> 00:04:17.249
Don’t edit its columns.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:19.053
Step 2

00:04:19.353 --> 00:04:22.228
Open Importer, pick your bank, and choose the file.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:24.072
Step 3

00:04:24.372 --> 00:04:28.632
Import, then read the inserted, re-indexed and unchanged counts.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:30.479
Step 4

00:04:30.779 --> 00:04:32.723
Check the new rows in Statements.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:34.764
Check your result

00:04:35.064 --> 00:04:38.531
The rows you expected are there, with the right dates and signs.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.359
Watch out

00:04:40.659 --> 00:04:44.011
If an import fails, check the bank you picked and the file.

00:04:44.191 --> 00:04:47.147
A PDF or a renamed spreadsheet won’t work.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:48.860
Example

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:54.689
Alex imports a salary of +$3,000 and a lunch of −$12.

00:04:54.869 --> 00:04:58.351
Neither one is explained until Alex creates Records.

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:01.730
Explaining your first purchase

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:03.261
The problem

00:05:03.561 --> 00:05:09.419
The lunch Statement says −$12, but the Dashboard only counts what a Record explains.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.222
The idea

00:05:11.522 --> 00:05:14.638
Allocator lists the bank amounts you haven’t explained yet.

00:05:14.818 --> 00:05:18.858
Pick one and create a Record with a title, an amount and a Category.

00:05:19.038 --> 00:05:21.554
Finpoint suggests the Allocation for you.

00:05:22.433 --> 00:05:23.366
Step 1

00:05:23.666 --> 00:05:26.293
Open Allocator and pick the lunch Statement.

00:05:27.167 --> 00:05:28.120
Step 2

00:05:28.420 --> 00:05:30.138
Create a Record called Lunch:

00:05:30.318 --> 00:05:32.686
Paid $12, in a food Category.

00:05:33.567 --> 00:05:34.539
Step 3

00:05:34.839 --> 00:05:38.189
Check the Allocation is −$12, then save.

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:40.231
Check your result

00:05:40.531 --> 00:05:44.062
The Record is complete, and the lunch Statement leaves Allocator.

00:05:44.933 --> 00:05:45.892
Watch out

00:05:46.192 --> 00:05:52.082
If the Record says Pending, compare its amount with its Allocations, and fix whichever is wrong.

00:05:52.933 --> 00:05:53.793
Example

00:05:54.093 --> 00:06:00.203
−$12 from the bank, −$12 allocated, a −$12 Lunch.

00:06:00.383 --> 00:06:03.084
Nothing left to allocate, and no difference.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:05.958
Protecting your work

00:06:06.558 --> 00:06:07.489
The problem

00:06:07.789 --> 00:06:09.941
Your data is saved in this browser.

00:06:10.121 --> 00:06:13.788
Clearing browser storage, or changing devices, can lose it.

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:15.588
The idea

00:06:15.888 --> 00:06:20.696
A Finpoint backup is a separate copy of your data, saved as a JSON file.

00:06:20.876 --> 00:06:22.395
The browser downloads it.

00:06:22.575 --> 00:06:26.424
Requesting a download isn’t proof, so find the file yourself.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.233
Step 1

00:06:28.533 --> 00:06:30.524
In Sync, choose Download backup.

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.353
Step 2

00:06:32.653 --> 00:06:35.109
Find the file, and keep it somewhere safe.

00:06:35.967 --> 00:06:36.939
Step 3

00:06:37.239 --> 00:06:39.989
Keep an older backup before you restore anything.

00:06:40.867 --> 00:06:42.031
Check your result

00:06:42.331 --> 00:06:43.876
You’ve found the backup file.

00:06:44.056 --> 00:06:46.536
Clicking the button alone doesn’t confirm that.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:48.359
Watch out

00:06:48.659 --> 00:06:52.393
Never clear browser storage until you have a backup you’ve checked.

00:06:53.267 --> 00:06:54.126
Example

00:06:54.426 --> 00:06:57.559
After explaining Lunch, Alex exports a backup.

00:06:57.739 --> 00:07:00.768
Restoring it later replaces what’s in this browser.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:03.804
Splitting one payment

00:07:04.404 --> 00:07:05.335
The problem

00:07:05.635 --> 00:07:12.541
One −$80 supermarket Statement was really $60 of groceries and a $20 gift.

00:07:12.721 --> 00:07:15.782
Put it all in one Category, and that difference hides.

00:07:16.633 --> 00:07:17.555
The idea

00:07:17.855 --> 00:07:21.584
Create two Records, and allocate part of the Statement to each.

00:07:21.764 --> 00:07:25.424
The Statement stays whole, and you get two clear explanations.

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.233
Step 1

00:07:27.533 --> 00:07:32.426
Create Groceries for −$60, from the −$80 Statement.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:34.253
Step 2

00:07:34.553 --> 00:07:36.487
−$20 is left.

00:07:36.667 --> 00:07:39.906
Create Gift for −$20, and allocate it.

00:07:40.767 --> 00:07:41.739
Step 3

00:07:42.039 --> 00:07:45.257
Check both Records, and that $0 remains.

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:47.297
Check your result

00:07:47.597 --> 00:07:52.528
−$60 plus −$20 equals −$80.

00:07:52.708 --> 00:07:56.173
Both Records are complete, and the Statement is fully used.

00:07:57.033 --> 00:07:57.992
Watch out

00:07:58.292 --> 00:08:00.975
You can’t allocate more than a Statement has left.

00:08:01.155 --> 00:08:02.538
Fix the Allocations;

00:08:02.718 --> 00:08:04.549
don’t invent bank activity.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.260
Example

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:14.077
One Statement of −$80 becomes Groceries −$60 and Gift −$20.

00:08:14.257 --> 00:08:16.964
One Statement, two useful explanations.

00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:20.303
A shared dinner and repayment

00:08:20.903 --> 00:08:21.834
The problem

00:08:22.134 --> 00:08:24.349
Alex pays $90 for dinner.

00:08:24.529 --> 00:08:27.791
Later, Alex receives $60 back from friends.

00:08:27.971 --> 00:08:31.520
Alex’s own cost is $30, not $90.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:33.322
The idea

00:08:33.622 --> 00:08:37.480
One Shared dinner Record can combine the payment and the repayment.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:39.283
The money back isn’t income.

00:08:39.463 --> 00:08:41.207
It reduces Alex’s share.

00:08:42.067 --> 00:08:42.999
Step 1

00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:45.850
Create Shared dinner for −$30.

00:08:46.733 --> 00:08:47.686
Step 2

00:08:47.986 --> 00:08:51.064
Allocate −$90 from the dinner payment.

00:08:51.933 --> 00:08:52.905
Step 3

00:08:53.205 --> 00:08:55.812
Allocate +$60 from the repayment.

00:08:56.667 --> 00:08:57.831
Check your result

00:08:58.131 --> 00:09:02.686
−$90 + $60 = −$30.

00:09:02.866 --> 00:09:06.264
Both Statements are explained, and the Record is complete.

00:09:07.133 --> 00:09:08.092
Watch out

00:09:08.392 --> 00:09:11.742
Before the repayment arrives, the Record may show Pending.

00:09:11.922 --> 00:09:13.980
That alone doesn’t mean someone owes you.

00:09:14.833 --> 00:09:15.693
Example

00:09:15.993 --> 00:09:22.162
Payment −$90 and repayment +$60 go into one Shared dinner Record.

00:09:22.342 --> 00:09:24.940
It shows Alex’s −$30 share.

00:09:26.567 --> 00:09:28.742
Refunds, transfers and savings

00:09:29.342 --> 00:09:30.273
The problem

00:09:30.573 --> 00:09:36.725
A plus or minus sign doesn’t tell you whether money was income, spending, savings or a transfer.

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:38.522
The idea

00:09:38.822 --> 00:09:41.615
Treatment tells the Dashboard how to count a Record.

00:09:41.795 --> 00:09:43.260
Income is for salary.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:46.154
Spending covers purchases and their refunds.

00:09:46.334 --> 00:09:49.770
Saving or investment is money to and from savings.

00:09:49.950 --> 00:09:53.813
Transfer or neutral is money moving between your own Accounts.

00:09:53.993 --> 00:09:56.009
Automatic just follows the sign.

00:09:56.867 --> 00:09:57.799
Step 1

00:09:58.099 --> 00:10:02.078
Use Income for salary, and Spending for purchases and refunds.

00:10:02.933 --> 00:10:03.886
Step 2

00:10:04.186 --> 00:10:13.121
For a transfer, combine the −$200 and +$200 into one $0 Record, as Transfer or neutral.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:14.972
Step 3

00:10:15.272 --> 00:10:19.211
Use Saving or investment for contributions and withdrawals.

00:10:20.067 --> 00:10:21.231
Check your result

00:10:21.531 --> 00:10:24.773
A +$20 refund lowers your net spending.

00:10:24.953 --> 00:10:29.149
Saving or investment contributions show as positive amounts.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:30.959
Watch out

00:10:31.259 --> 00:10:37.358
A −$250 saving net means more cash went into savings than came out.

00:10:37.538 --> 00:10:39.301
It isn’t an investment loss.

00:10:40.167 --> 00:10:41.026
Example

00:10:41.326 --> 00:10:44.705
Put in $300 and take out $50:

00:10:44.885 --> 00:10:51.461
contributions $300, withdrawals $50, net −$250.

00:10:51.641 --> 00:10:55.010
The +$3,000 salary is separate income.

00:10:56.633 --> 00:10:58.070
Why a Record is Pending

00:10:58.670 --> 00:10:59.601
The problem

00:10:59.901 --> 00:11:06.810
A Record is Pending when its amounts don’t match, its Allocations are missing, or one of its Statements is Pending.

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:08.588
The idea

00:11:08.888 --> 00:11:12.598
A Pending Record is an explanation that doesn’t add up yet.

00:11:12.778 --> 00:11:17.612
A Pending Statement is a placeholder for bank activity you’re still waiting for.

00:11:17.792 --> 00:11:23.074
Even if the amounts match, the Record stays Pending until that placeholder is replaced.

00:11:23.933 --> 00:11:24.866
Step 1

00:11:25.166 --> 00:11:28.760
Open the Record and compare its amount with its Allocations.

00:11:29.633 --> 00:11:30.586
Step 2

00:11:30.886 --> 00:11:37.189
If lunch really cost $12, change the Record from −$10 to −$12.

00:11:38.067 --> 00:11:39.039
Step 3

00:11:39.339 --> 00:11:44.713
When the real Statement arrives, replace the Pending Statement instead of allocating twice.

00:11:45.567 --> 00:11:46.731
Check your result

00:11:47.031 --> 00:11:53.181
There’s at least one Allocation, the total matches the Record, and no allocated Statement is Pending.

00:11:54.033 --> 00:11:54.992
Watch out

00:11:55.292 --> 00:11:57.322
Fix the facts, not just the label.

00:11:57.502 --> 00:11:59.500
A Pending Record is still saved.

00:12:00.367 --> 00:12:01.226
Example

00:12:01.526 --> 00:12:06.505
The Record says −$10, but the Allocation is −$12.

00:12:06.685 --> 00:12:11.671
Change the Record to −$12, and the difference drops to $0.

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:15.004
Finding and fixing mistakes

00:12:15.604 --> 00:12:16.535
The problem

00:12:16.835 --> 00:12:19.068
Something you saved seems to be missing.

00:12:19.248 --> 00:12:21.857
Usually filters or the month are hiding it.

00:12:22.037 --> 00:12:24.713
An empty list doesn’t mean your data is gone.

00:12:25.567 --> 00:12:26.488
The idea

00:12:26.788 --> 00:12:28.665
Records show your explanations.

00:12:28.845 --> 00:12:31.051
Statements show all your bank activity.

00:12:31.231 --> 00:12:33.967
Allocator only shows amounts left to explain.

00:12:34.147 --> 00:12:36.904
Use search and filters to narrow the right list.

00:12:37.767 --> 00:12:38.699
Step 1

00:12:38.999 --> 00:12:41.901
Check the month and date range, and clear filters.

00:12:42.767 --> 00:12:43.720
Step 2

00:12:44.020 --> 00:12:47.258
Look in Statements for rows Allocator no longer shows.

00:12:48.133 --> 00:12:49.105
Step 3

00:12:49.405 --> 00:12:52.668
Open the item, edit it, then check its totals again.

00:12:53.533 --> 00:12:54.697
Check your result

00:12:54.997 --> 00:12:58.950
The fixed item shows up where you expect, still linked to its Statement.

00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:00.759
Watch out

00:13:01.059 --> 00:13:03.128
Renaming doesn’t fix a wrong amount.

00:13:03.308 --> 00:13:05.454
Keep a backup before big changes.

00:13:06.333 --> 00:13:07.193
Example

00:13:07.493 --> 00:13:11.415
To find payments over $50, choose Paid and type 50.

00:13:11.595 --> 00:13:13.170
No minus sign needed.

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.831
Category, treatment and bucket

00:13:17.431 --> 00:13:18.362
The problem

00:13:18.662 --> 00:13:20.860
Three settings describe every Record.

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.407
Mixing them up makes totals confusing.

00:13:24.267 --> 00:13:25.188
The idea

00:13:25.488 --> 00:13:27.194
Category is what it’s for.

00:13:27.374 --> 00:13:29.455
Treatment is how totals count it.

00:13:29.635 --> 00:13:33.573
A bucket is an optional planning group, like Daily or Recurring.

00:13:33.753 --> 00:13:37.434
A Category can set defaults, and a Record can override them.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.233
Step 1

00:13:39.533 --> 00:13:42.038
In Categories, create the labels you need.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:43.853
Step 2

00:13:44.153 --> 00:13:47.322
Give each Category a treatment and a default bucket.

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:49.172
Step 3

00:13:49.472 --> 00:13:52.685
On a Record, check whether those defaults were overridden.

00:13:53.567 --> 00:13:54.731
Check your result

00:13:55.031 --> 00:13:58.213
Lunch can be Food, Spending and Daily all at once:

00:13:58.393 --> 00:13:59.807
three different choices.

00:14:00.667 --> 00:14:01.625
Watch out

00:14:01.925 --> 00:14:06.801
Changing a Category doesn’t update Records whose choices were set by hand.

00:14:07.667 --> 00:14:08.526
Example

00:14:08.826 --> 00:14:09.603
Lunch:

00:14:09.783 --> 00:14:12.718
Category Food, treatment Spending, bucket Daily.

00:14:12.898 --> 00:14:15.821
Rent is Spending too, but in the Recurring bucket.

00:14:17.433 --> 00:14:18.704
Monthly targets

00:14:19.304 --> 00:14:20.236
The problem

00:14:20.536 --> 00:14:24.637
You want to compare a month’s spending with a plan, without moving any money.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:26.422
The idea

00:14:26.722 --> 00:14:29.862
A target is a monthly comparison for a bucket.

00:14:30.042 --> 00:14:32.614
A default carries forward from the month you set it.

00:14:32.794 --> 00:14:35.332
A target for only one month overrides it.

00:14:35.512 --> 00:14:38.322
Targets never reserve cash or block spending.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:40.133
Step 1

00:14:40.433 --> 00:14:42.581
On the Dashboard, pick the month first.

00:14:43.433 --> 00:14:44.386
Step 2

00:14:44.686 --> 00:14:47.652
Choose this month only, or from this month onward.

00:14:48.533 --> 00:14:49.505
Step 3

00:14:49.805 --> 00:14:52.164
Compare the bucket’s spending with its target.

00:14:53.033 --> 00:14:54.197
Check your result

00:14:54.497 --> 00:15:02.258
Daily spending of $127 against a $500 target leaves $373.

00:15:03.133 --> 00:15:03.993
Example

00:15:04.293 --> 00:15:08.167
A $500 default starts in October and carries on.

00:15:08.347 --> 00:15:15.409
A $600 target just for October wins in October, and later months go back to $500.

00:15:17.033 --> 00:15:18.795
Budgets for trips and events

00:15:19.395 --> 00:15:20.326
The problem

00:15:20.626 --> 00:15:23.523
A trip can cross months and several categories.

00:15:23.703 --> 00:15:25.644
A monthly bucket doesn’t fit it.

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:27.422
The idea

00:15:27.722 --> 00:15:32.230
A Budget has its own amount, its own dates, and its own attached Records.

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:34.933
It’s a separate view from the Dashboard’s totals.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:36.733
Step 1

00:15:37.033 --> 00:15:39.949
In Budgets, create one with an amount and dates.

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:41.753
Step 2

00:15:42.053 --> 00:15:46.296
Leave Automatic attach on, or turn it off to choose Records yourself.

00:15:47.167 --> 00:15:48.139
Step 3

00:15:48.439 --> 00:15:51.451
After changing dates, review the attached Records.

00:15:52.333 --> 00:15:53.497
Check your result

00:15:53.797 --> 00:15:57.562
The trip’s Records are attached, and fall inside the Budget’s dates.

00:15:58.433 --> 00:15:59.392
Watch out

00:15:59.692 --> 00:16:02.979
A Budget total doesn’t have to match Dashboard spending.

00:16:03.833 --> 00:16:04.693
Example

00:16:04.993 --> 00:16:07.386
Use a Budget for a week-long holiday.

00:16:07.566 --> 00:16:10.017
Use the Travel bucket for the monthly view.

00:16:11.633 --> 00:16:12.872
Reading the Dashboard

00:16:13.472 --> 00:16:14.403
The problem

00:16:14.703 --> 00:16:19.655
The charts depend on the month and on your Records, so they won’t match your bank balances.

00:16:20.533 --> 00:16:21.455
The idea

00:16:21.755 --> 00:16:26.625
The Dashboard summarizes your Records by treatment, then by Category and bucket.

00:16:26.805 --> 00:16:30.219
Monthly Records shows the Records behind each number.

00:16:30.399 --> 00:16:33.526
Pending Records already count, so tidy them first.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:35.333
Step 1

00:16:35.633 --> 00:16:36.633
Pick the month.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:38.453
Step 2

00:16:38.753 --> 00:16:41.829
Read income, spending, refunds and surplus.

00:16:42.700 --> 00:16:43.672
Step 3

00:16:43.972 --> 00:16:46.666
Use Monthly Records to trace any surprise.

00:16:47.533 --> 00:16:48.697
Check your result

00:16:48.997 --> 00:16:51.023
Income is $3,000.

00:16:51.203 --> 00:16:59.553
Gross spending is $147, minus $20 of refunds, so net spending is $127.

00:16:59.733 --> 00:17:03.003
Surplus is $2,873.

00:17:03.867 --> 00:17:04.825
Watch out

00:17:05.125 --> 00:17:09.072
If a number looks wrong, check the month first, then the Record’s treatment.

00:17:09.933 --> 00:17:10.793
Example

00:17:11.093 --> 00:17:15.555
A saving net of −$250 shows on its own line.

00:17:15.735 --> 00:17:18.693
It doesn’t mean income fell, or that you lost money.

00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:21.894
Backups and Google Drive

00:17:22.494 --> 00:17:23.425
The problem

00:17:23.725 --> 00:17:28.746
With two devices, you need a copy you can trust, and to know which copy is current.

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:30.522
The idea

00:17:30.822 --> 00:17:32.501
Your data lives locally.

00:17:32.681 --> 00:17:34.812
A backup file is a separate copy.

00:17:34.992 --> 00:17:39.643
Google Drive sync is optional, and shares one workspace across devices.

00:17:39.823 --> 00:17:43.051
The sign-in broker never sees your financial data.

00:17:43.933 --> 00:17:44.866
Step 1

00:17:45.166 --> 00:17:47.955
In Sync, download a backup and find the file.

00:17:48.833 --> 00:17:49.786
Step 2

00:17:50.086 --> 00:17:52.661
Connect Google Drive only if you want sync.

00:17:53.533 --> 00:17:54.505
Step 3

00:17:54.805 --> 00:17:58.712
If there’s a conflict, back up first, then choose which copy to keep.

00:17:59.567 --> 00:18:00.731
Check your result

00:18:01.031 --> 00:18:03.783
Up to date in Sync, and a backup you’ve found.

00:18:03.963 --> 00:18:04.936
Keep both.

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:06.759
Watch out

00:18:07.059 --> 00:18:10.416
Don’t keep overwriting a conflict just to clear the warning.

00:18:10.596 --> 00:18:12.063
You choose a whole copy;

00:18:12.243 --> 00:18:13.588
nothing is merged.

00:18:14.467 --> 00:18:15.326
Example

00:18:15.626 --> 00:18:20.517
Your practice progress stays in this browser, without going into your backup or Drive.

00:18:22.133 --> 00:18:23.156
Your routine

00:18:23.756 --> 00:18:24.687
The problem

00:18:24.987 --> 00:18:27.244
Don’t try to perfect everything at once.

00:18:27.424 --> 00:18:29.182
A short routine works better.

00:18:30.033 --> 00:18:30.955
The idea

00:18:31.255 --> 00:18:32.250
Each visit:

00:18:32.430 --> 00:18:39.663
import new activity, explain what’s left, inspect anything Pending, review the month, and protect it all with a backup.

00:18:40.533 --> 00:18:41.466
Step 1

00:18:41.766 --> 00:18:43.886
Import your latest bank CSV.

00:18:44.767 --> 00:18:45.720
Step 2

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.119
Explain new amounts in Allocator.

00:18:49.000 --> 00:18:49.972
Step 3

00:18:50.272 --> 00:18:52.141
Review the month, then check Sync.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.164
Check your result

00:18:54.464 --> 00:18:59.487
You can follow any bank amount to its explanation, and get your work back if you need it.

00:19:00.367 --> 00:19:01.226
Example

00:19:01.526 --> 00:19:07.336
Import, explain one purchase, fix one Pending Record, review the month, and check your backup.
